    "text": "To solve the problem, we need to use the properties of the angles in a trapezoid and the fact that they form an arithmetic sequence.\n\nFirst, let's denote the four angles of the trapezoid as \\(a\\), \\(a+d\\), \\(a+2d\\), and \\(a+3d\\), where \\(a\\) is the smallest angle and \\(d\\) is the common difference of the arithmetic sequence. We are given that the smallest angle \\(a = 60^\\circ\\).\n\nSince the sum of the interior angles of any quadrilateral is \\(360^\\circ\\), we can write the equation:\n\\[\na + (a+d) + (a+2d) + (a+3d) = 360^\\circ\n\\]\nSubstituting \\(a = 60^\\circ\\) into the equation, we get:\n\\[\n60^\\circ + (60^\\circ + d) + (60^\\circ + 2d) + (60^\\circ + 3d) = 360^\\circ\n\\]\nSimplifying the left side, we have:\n\\[\n60^\\circ + 60^\\circ + 60^\\circ + 60^\\circ + d + 2d + 3d = 360^\\circ\n\\]\n\\[\n240^\\circ + 6d = 360^\\circ\n\\]\nSubtracting \\(240^\\circ\\) from both sides, we get:\n\\[\n6d = 120^\\circ\n\\]\nDividing both sides by 6, we find:\n\\[\nd = 20^\\circ\n\\]\nNow that we have the common difference \\(d\\), we can find the measure of the largest angle, which is \\(a + 3d\\):\n\\[\na + 3d = 60^\\circ + 3 \\cdot 20^\\circ = 60^\\circ + 60^\\circ = 120^\\circ\n\\]\nThus, the measure of the largest angle is \\(\\boxed{120}\\).",
